    "knowledge_point": "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring",
    "problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x}=0?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-1)\\cdot {7}^{2-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2-x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-1=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-1,1.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2-x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2-x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-1,1,$ and their product is $-1\\cdot 1=-1.$",
